From ae0130b89b4beb333f8bf6c1cd5f392de1474e31 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nova <nova@papi-ux.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 17:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] fix(video): keep ENet's throttle from discarding reception
 reports

Nova #442 follow-up. Stock ENet sends an unreliable packet only while its
rotating packetThrottleCounter (+7 per packet, mod 32) does not exceed
packetThrottle, and lowers packetThrottle by the deceleration (2) for
every acknowledgement slower than the previous 5 s epoch's lowest RTT
plus twice its variance. The rest are freed inside enet_host_service()
after enet_peer_send() already succeeded, so an RTT spike silently
erased runs of 0x7F10 reports while the reliable ping, video and the
report clock all continued. At throttle 0 only a packet whose counter is
0 survives: every 32nd report when reports are the peer's only
unreliable traffic.

Only for a control peer whose negotiated extension actually started
timing (PacketReceptionIsTimed(): started, clock valid, not retired),
startControlStream() now calls enet_peer_throttle_configure() with the
peer's current interval and acceleration and zero deceleration, under
enetMutex, after the observer starts and before the loss-stats thread
exists. The API changes only future deceleration, and the START A/B
acknowledgements may already have lowered the throttle, so a lowered
packetThrottle is restored to packetThrottleLimit, the same assignment
ENet makes in its own stable-RTT branch. Old, absent, refused,
duplicated, withdrawn, mutex-failed and clock-failed peers keep stock
ENet throttling and send nothing new. If the extension retires while the
stream continues (clock sentinel or sequence limit) the loss-stats
thread restores the previous deceleration; stop closes publication
before retiring the collector and never sends it. A new connection
starts from ENet's defaults.

This is peer-wide, not report-only: unreliable frame-FEC status,
controller motion and touch or pen hover/move input on this peer are no
longer RTT-throttled either, the reliable window is no longer scaled
down by the throttle, and THROTTLE_CONFIGURE (an ENet protocol command
outside the encrypted control payloads) gives the host's peer the same
zero deceleration. Reports stay unreliable and unsequenced, at most one
per existing tick, with no retry, queue or catch-up; wire loss still
loses them.

The control stream logs one setup row per connection and one row per
ten report sequences (about one a second): throttle, limit, counter,
interval, acceleration, deceleration, RTT and variance, read under
enetMutex. No address, identity, key, nonce or payload is logged. Test
builds add a configure switch, a pre-configure hook and a reader.
---
 src/ControlStream.c   | 166 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 src/PacketReception.c |  24 ++++++
 src/PacketReception.h |  27 +++++++
 3 files changed, 210 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

